  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Regular forms",
     "texto": [
      "The starting point is the **eles** form of the simple pretérito perfeito, and the ending **-ram** changes to **-sse**: **falaram** → **falasse** (spoke), **comeram** → **comesse** (ate), **partiram** → **partisse** (left).",
      "With **eu** and **tu** too: **falasse**, **falasses**; with **ele**, **falasse**."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Irregular forms",
     "texto": [
      "**ter** → **tivesse** (had), **ser** → **fosse** (were), **estar** → **estivesse** (were), **fazer** → **fizesse** (did), **dizer** → **dissesse** (said), **poder** → **pudesse** (could), **ver** → **visse** (saw), **dar** → **desse** (gave).",
      "Watch out: **ser** and **ir** share the form **fosse**."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Unreal condition",
     "texto": [
      "To imagine something that doesn't happen: **Se eu tivesse tempo, viajaria** (if I had time, I would travel). The condition takes **se + imperfect subjunctive** and the result usually takes the **conditional**. In European Portuguese the indicative imperfect is also common, especially in speech: **Se tivesse tempo, viajava**. The conditional is not used in the clause introduced by **se**."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
